WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.300
Core OT security begins with asset inventory.

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:12.648
Record devices, software, firmware, communication paths, owners, vendors, physical locations, operational functions, and critical dependencies.

00:00:12.648 --> 00:00:16.949
Inventory is not merely a spreadsheet exercise.

00:00:16.949 --> 00:00:24.285
It supports segmentation, monitoring, vulnerability decisions, incident response, backup planning, and lifecycle management.

00:00:24.285 --> 00:00:28.585
Secure remote access through managed entry points.

00:00:28.585 --> 00:00:36.933
Use individual identities, strong authentication, least privilege, approval, time limits, and session monitoring where feasible.

00:00:36.933 --> 00:00:40.728
Remove dormant pathways and shared credentials.

00:00:40.728 --> 00:00:48.570
Coordinate emergency access procedures in advance so operational urgency does not force insecure improvisation.

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:53.882
Network monitoring provides visibility without necessarily touching sensitive devices.

00:00:53.882 --> 00:01:05.266
Establish a baseline of expected communications, then investigate new connections, unusual protocols, unauthorized devices, configuration transfers, and traffic crossing trust boundaries.

00:01:05.266 --> 00:01:14.120
Monitoring must be interpreted with operational context; maintenance activity may look unusual but be fully authorized.

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:21.456
Segmentation limits pathways, while access control limits who and what can use them.

00:01:21.456 --> 00:01:27.274
Apply role-based privileges to operator, engineering, administrative, and vendor functions.

00:01:27.274 --> 00:01:31.068
Protect privileged workstations and management interfaces.

00:01:31.068 --> 00:01:36.381
Review accounts when personnel, contracts, and job responsibilities change.

00:01:36.381 --> 00:01:47.258
Backups should cover controller logic, device configurations, HMI and SCADA applications, historian settings, servers, and the documentation needed to rebuild.

00:01:47.258 --> 00:01:52.065
Store protected copies, verify integrity, and practice restoration.

00:01:52.065 --> 00:02:02.436
Recovery plans must account for physical process state and the order in which systems can safely return to service.

00:02:02.436 --> 00:02:11.796
Vulnerability management in OT combines vendor advisories, asset context, exposure, compensating controls, test results, and maintenance planning.

00:02:11.796 --> 00:02:22.168
Change control ensures that patches, configuration updates, firewall rules, and logic changes are reviewed, tested, authorized, documented, and reversible.

00:02:22.168 --> 00:02:32.034
Finally, incident response plans should define roles for security personnel, operators, engineers, safety teams, leadership, communications, and vendors.

00:02:32.034 --> 00:02:38.864
Practice scenarios involving loss of visibility, remote-access misuse, ransomware, and control-system disruption.

00:02:38.864 --> 00:02:48.224
The goal is coordinated decision-making that protects people and operations while preserving evidence and restoring trusted control.
